quick_facts: 1 W = 1 joule per second
quick_facts: Named after James Watt (honored 1889, formalized in SI)
quick_facts: Plants quote MWt (heat) and MWe (electricity)
quick_facts: Typical large unit: ~3,000 MWt → ~1,000 MWe
quick_facts: ~33% efficiency set by steam-cycle thermodynamics

sources:
    {"organization":"BIPM","title":"SI Units – watt","url":"https://www.bipm.org/en/measurement-units"}

definition:
    The SI unit of power — one joule per second — named for Scottish engineer James Watt, whose steam engines powered the first industrial revolution. Nuclear plants are rated two ways that matter: thermal megawatts describe heat from the core (a typical large unit: ~3,000 MWt), while electrical megawatts describe what reaches the grid (~1,000 MWe) — the gap being thermodynamic efficiency. A single large reactor thus powers around a million homes continuously, day and night.
